    <h1>Records and Types</h1>
    <p class="article-meta" data-pagefind-ignore>
      <span>6 min read</span>
    </p>
    <p>A record type describes the fields a value supplies. GCSL uses structural
types: two declarations with the same structure describe the same type,
and a record can be used where fewer of its fields are required.</p>
<h2 id="declaring-a-shape">Declaring a shape <a class="heading-anchor" href="#declaring-a-shape" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Use <code>type</code> to give a structure a reusable name. An annotation checks a
value against that structure. Defaults supply omitted fields in a record
literal checked against the type:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>type Install = {from: String, exclude: List&lt;String&gt; = []}
let item: Install = {from = "src"}
item.exclude
</code></pre></div>
<p>Defaults are constant expressions, not arbitrary function calls. They are
applied where a record literal has the expected type; they don't add
fields to an already constructed variable with a smaller type.</p>
<p>An annotation also determines what fields later code can see. Extra
fields in a record literal produce a warning when an expected type hides
them. A structural alias does not introduce a distinct runtime identity.</p>
<h2 id="generic-types">Generic types <a class="heading-anchor" href="#generic-types" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>A type can take type parameters, named in angle brackets after its name.
Each use names the types to put in their place:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>type Pair&lt;A, B&gt; = {first: A, second: B}
let entry: Pair&lt;String, Int&gt; = {first = "port"; second = 8080}
entry.second
</code></pre></div>
<p>A use must give as many type arguments as the type has parameters, and a
type without parameters takes none. Because types are structural,
<code>Pair&lt;String, Int&gt;</code> is the same type as <code>{first: String, second: Int}</code>. A
generic type can be built from another, as in
<code>type Named&lt;T&gt; = Pair&lt;String, T&gt;</code>. It can't refer to itself, and it can't
spread one of its parameters, because the parameter might not be a record.</p>
<h2 id="enums">Enums <a class="heading-anchor" href="#enums" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>An extensible enum is a type whose variants any module can add. Declare
the enum, with an optional bound that every variant's payload must fit,
and then its variants, each with a payload type:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>enum Shape extensible&lt;{*: Int}&gt;
enum Shape::Square&lt;{side: Int}&gt;
enum Shape::Point
square = Shape::Square({side = 3})
</code></pre></div>
<p>A variant is also a value: <code>Shape::Square</code> on its own has the type
<code>Variant&lt;Shape, {side: Int}&gt;</code>. Calling it makes a value of the enum,
and you can pass it to a function as a key. <code>kind.matches(value)</code> tests
whether a value is of that variant, and <code>kind.payload(value)</code> takes out
its payload:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>enum Tool extensible
enum Tool::C&lt;{cc: String}&gt;
fn find&lt;T&gt;(kind: Variant&lt;Tool, T&gt;, tools: List&lt;Tool&gt;, fallback: T): T {
    for t in tools { if kind.matches(t) { return kind.payload(t) } }
    fallback
}
cc = find(Tool::C, [Tool::C({cc = "gcc"})], {cc = "cc"}).cc
</code></pre></div>
<p>Unlike records, enums are nominal: two enums are different types even
if they look alike. <code>value is Tool</code> tests whether a value is of the enum.
Values of an enum are compared with <code>==</code> only when the enum has a bound,
because an unbounded payload might hold a function. A variant is visible
in its own file, to the other files of its directory and the directories
below it, and to every file that imports its module.</p>
<h2 id="partial-records">Partial records <a class="heading-anchor" href="#partial-records" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p><code>Partial&lt;T&gt;</code> holds any of the fields of the record type <code>T</code>. Use it to
describe a record by only some of its fields, and <code>.matches()</code> to test
a whole record against it:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>type Platform = {arch: String, os: String}
let wanted: Partial&lt;Platform&gt; = {arch = "x86_64"}
fits = wanted.matches({arch = "x86_64"; os = "linux"})
</code></pre></div>
<p>A field of a partial record can't be read by name, because it may be
absent.</p>
<h2 id="records-of-any-fields">Records of any fields <a class="heading-anchor" href="#records-of-any-fields" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>A rest-field, <code>*: T</code>, gives the type of every field that a record type
doesn't name. Use it when the field names are data, such as paths. A
<code>{*: String}</code> holds any fields, each a <code>String</code>. Named fields keep their
own types, so <code>{to: String, *: Int}</code> holds a <code>String</code> field <code>to</code>, and any
other fields are <code>Int</code>s:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let files: {*: String} = {"bin/*" = "bin/*"; "sbin/*" = "sbin/*"}
files
</code></pre></div>
<p>Write the record literal where the type is expected: in an annotation,
an argument or a definition's result. There, each field that the type
doesn't name is checked against the rest-field. A value whose own type
has no rest-field doesn't fit one, because a record keeps fields that its
static type hides, and they could be of any type.</p>
<p>Read a named field as usual. A field that only the rest-field covers
can't be read by name, and a record whose type has a rest-field can't be
spread. Typed equality compares every field, and <code>is</code> and <code>as</code> test each
field that the rest-field covers.</p>
<h2 id="combining-fields">Combining fields <a class="heading-anchor" href="#combining-fields" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Dotted definitions construct nested records. Compatible dotted paths
merge, including across files in a workspace:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>server.host = "localhost"
server.port = 8080
server.tls.enabled = true
</code></pre></div>
<p>A whole-field definition such as <code>server = {...}</code> closes that field to
further dotted extensions. Define the individual dotted leaves when you
want several files to contribute to a shared structure.</p>
<p>A spread starts from another record's visible fields. Inside an ordinary
record block, put defaults first and explicit overrides afterward:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let defaults = {host = "localhost"; port = 80}
let config = {
    ...defaults
    port = 8080
}
config.port
</code></pre></div>
<p>A spread copies only fields visible through its operand's static type.
It cannot replace an earlier explicit field inside an ordinary block.
Top-level declarations are resolved across the whole input and take
precedence over a spread of the same name regardless of textual order.</p>
<h2 id="unions-and-narrowing">Unions and narrowing <a class="heading-anchor" href="#unions-and-narrowing" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>A union, such as <code>Int | String</code>, allows more than one shape. Use <code>is</code> to
test a value and <code>as</code> for a checked conversion that fails at runtime if
the value doesn't fit. In <code>if name is Type</code>, the branches see narrowed
copies of the named value. This does not perform general flow analysis of
later statements or dotted field paths.</p>
<p>Typed equality <code>==</code> compares operands of the same static type. Total
equality <code>===</code> compares complete runtime records, including fields hidden by
their shared static type. Both require the same static operand type. The
language's
restrictions on unions, functions, and futures are described in
<code>gcsl(5)</code> and <a href="/gcsl/language/inference-limits">Inference Limits</a>.</p>

